-- READS-DIFFERENTLY-ON-MAIN: 7 of 44 traces class differently on the stable binary (main) from the unstable one (the EXPECT above each is the unstable class).
-- The cause in every case is one: when the clock of a MAY runs out (a late act, or none), main does not close the power; its result is the next step that follows a use of the power
-- (the court's step in sections 338 and 341(ב), the dissenter's sale and the offeror's purchase in 341(א)) left open, so it prints RESIDUAL where the unstable binary takes the LEST branch and ends FULFILLED.
-- All seven are lapses or late acts; every in-time trace and every gate-fails trace gives the same class on both binaries.
-- line 118: unstable FULFILLED, main RESIDUAL: 338(ב): an application on day 182 is after the six months.
-- line 123: unstable FULFILLED, main RESIDUAL: 338(ב): no application by day 200.
-- line 133: unstable FULFILLED, main RESIDUAL: 338(ב): accepted on 31 August 2026: an application on day 182 is after the six months.
-- line 165: unstable FULFILLED, main RESIDUAL: 341(א): the offeror notifies on day 93, after the month.
-- line 175: unstable FULFILLED, main RESIDUAL: 341(א): offer on 30 December 2026: a notice on day 89 is after the month that follows the two months.
-- line 185: unstable FULFILLED, main RESIDUAL: 341(א): the gate holds and the offeror gives no notice by day 100.
-- line 230: unstable FULFILLED, main RESIDUAL: 341(ב),(ג): an application on day 29 is after the month, then the offeror sends on day 40 and the company registers on day 50.
-- One more trace matches on main for a different reason: line 237 (341(ג): no application by day 100) is RESIDUAL on both, but the open step is the offeror's duty on the unstable binary and the court's step on main,
-- because main takes the HENCE of the lapsed MAY.
